Build the DS core against devkitA64 GCC 15.2.0 and libnx 4.x.

melonDS is the last core still failing, and it failed for four different
reasons across four builds. All of them come from the same place: the
buildbot pins an older toolchain, this builds on the current one.

1. A real defect a lenient compiler had been waving through:

       rsemaphore.c:104: 'return' with no value, in function returning
       non-void

   ssem_trywait returns bool and its null check returned nothing. false is
   the only defensible value; every caller reads the result as "did I get
   it".

2. Declarations that used to arrive transitively and no longer do, which is
   libstdc++ tightening its own includes rather than melonDS changing:
   <ctime> for time(), <limits> for std::numeric_limits, and <algorithm>
   for std::min and std::max across five more files. Those five were found
   by scanning every source for a std symbol whose header it never
   includes, rather than waiting for each to surface a build at a time.

3. libnx 4.0's removal of virtmemReserve, which now makes melonDS the
   fourth core hit by it after mupen64plus, PPSSPP and flycast. There is
   only one call here and it feeds svcMapProcessMemory, so it wants general
   purpose address space; JitRXStart is found separately by scanning with
   svcQueryMemory and needs nothing. The reservation is held in a new member
   so the destructor can release it, where virtmemFree used to sit, since
   that call was a documented no-op and its replacement is not. The member
   forward declares VirtmemReservation rather than pulling <switch.h> into
   every translation unit including this header: a pointer to an incomplete
   type is all that is needed.

4. offsetof with a runtime index:

       ARMJIT_Compiler.cpp:236: 'reg' is not a constant expression

   offsetof(ARM, R[reg]) is undefined behaviour that compilers accepted for
   years and GCC 15 rejects. Rewritten as offsetof(ARM, R) + sizeof(u32) *
   reg, which is the same address by definition and actually legal. The
   offsetof(ARM, R[15]) a few lines away is fine and untouched: a constant
   index has always been valid.

Files belonging to the desktop frontend and the x64 JIT are deliberately
left alone. Neither is in an aarch64 libretro build.

diff --git a/src/ARMJIT_A64/ARMJIT_Compiler.cpp b/src/ARMJIT_A64/ARMJIT_Compiler.cpp
index 50e33fc..da296e5 100644
--- a/src/ARMJIT_A64/ARMJIT_Compiler.cpp
+++ b/src/ARMJIT_A64/ARMJIT_Compiler.cpp
@@ -1,3 +1,4 @@
+#include <algorithm>
 #include "ARMJIT_Compiler.h"
 
 #include "../ARMInterpreter.h"
@@ -52,7 +53,14 @@ Compiler::Compiler()
     JitRWBase = memalign(0x1000, JitMemSize);
 
     JitRXStart = (u8*)&__start__ - JitMemSize - 0x1000;
-    JitRWStart = virtmemReserve(JitMemSize);
+    /* libnx 4.0 removed virtmemReserve. This address is handed to
+       svcMapProcessMemory below, so it needs general purpose address space;
+       JitRXStart is found separately by scanning with svcQueryMemory and is
+       not affected. The 0x1000 guard matches the old call. */
+    virtmemLock();
+    JitRWStart = virtmemFindAslr(JitMemSize, 0x1000);
+    JitRWReservation = JitRWStart ? virtmemAddReservation(JitRWStart, JitMemSize) : NULL;
+    virtmemUnlock();
     MemoryInfo info = {0};
     u32 pageInfo = {0};
     int i = 0;
@@ -219,7 +227,15 @@ Compiler::~Compiler()
     {
         bool succeded = R_SUCCEEDED(svcUnmapProcessMemory(JitRWStart, envGetOwnProcessHandle(), (u64)JitRXStart, JitMemSize));
         assert(succeded);
-        virtmemFree(JitRWStart, JitMemSize);
+        /* virtmemFree was a documented no-op; a reservation is not, and
+           leaks the address space if it is never removed. */
+        if (JitRWReservation)
+        {
+            virtmemLock();
+            virtmemRemoveReservation(JitRWReservation);
+            virtmemUnlock();
+            JitRWReservation = NULL;
+        }
         succeded = R_SUCCEEDED(svcUnmapProcessCodeMemory(envGetOwnProcessHandle(), (u64)JitRXStart, (u64)JitRWBase, JitMemSize));
         assert(succeded);
         free(JitRWBase);
@@ -232,12 +248,12 @@ void Compiler::LoadReg(int reg, ARM64Reg nativeReg)
     if (reg == 15)
         MOVI2R(nativeReg, R15);
     else
-        LDR(INDEX_UNSIGNED, nativeReg, RCPU, offsetof(ARM, R[reg]));
+        LDR(INDEX_UNSIGNED, nativeReg, RCPU, offsetof(ARM, R) + sizeof(u32) * reg);
 }
 
 void Compiler::SaveReg(int reg, ARM64Reg nativeReg)
 {
-    STR(INDEX_UNSIGNED, nativeReg, RCPU, offsetof(ARM, R[reg]));
+    STR(INDEX_UNSIGNED, nativeReg, RCPU, offsetof(ARM, R) + sizeof(u32) * reg);
 }
 
 void Compiler::LoadCPSR()
diff --git a/src/ARMJIT_A64/ARMJIT_Compiler.h b/src/ARMJIT_A64/ARMJIT_Compiler.h
index 7e13507..37e57d4 100644
--- a/src/ARMJIT_A64/ARMJIT_Compiler.h
+++ b/src/ARMJIT_A64/ARMJIT_Compiler.h
@@ -225,6 +225,16 @@ public:
 #ifdef __SWITCH__
     void* JitRWBase;
     void* JitRWStart;
+#ifdef __SWITCH__
+    /* Forward declared rather than pulling <switch.h> into every translation
+       unit that includes this header; a pointer to an incomplete type is all
+       that is needed here. libnx declares it as
+       "typedef struct VirtmemReservation VirtmemReservation".
+
+       Held so the destructor can release what virtmemAddReservation took:
+       libnx 4.0's replacement for virtmemReserve is not a no-op to undo. */
+    struct VirtmemReservation* JitRWReservation;
+#endif
     void* JitRXStart;
 #endif
 };
